American singer Olivia Rodrigo became the latest global star to bring Palestinian craftsmanship into the fashion spotlight, wearing a handmade dress featuring traditional tatreez embroidery at her inaugural Daisy Chain Fields festival. She joined a list of other celebrities who have used fashion as a symbol for the Palestinian cause.
According to Khaleej Times, the Grammy-winning singer wore a custom pale pink mini dress by Palestinian fashion label N&ouml;l Collective during a press presentation ahead of the festival at Great Park in Irvine, California.
The handmade piece featured embroidered birds and interconnected daisy chains, a fitting nod to the women-led festival's name and wider themes. Olivia&nbsp;paired the look with vintage Gucci Mary Jane heels and a silver pendant.
N&ouml;l Collective, founded by Palestinian-American designer Yasmeen Mjalli,&nbsp;based in Ramallah, works with family-run sewing workshops, artisan studios and women's cooperatives across Palestine. The label places traditional craftsmanship, including tatreez embroidery, handweaving and natural dyeing, at the centre of its work.
While the singer's dress drew attention for its craftsmanship, she is not the first public figure to use fashion as a means to draw attention to Palestinian culture and heritage amid the region's ongoing crisis.
Bella Hadid and the keffiyeh dress
Few celebrities have brought Palestinian heritage to the fashion conversation as consistently as model Bella Hadid.
At the 2024 Cannes Film Festival, the Palestinian-Dutch supermodel wore a vintage red-and-white dress made from keffiyeh fabric, transforming one of the world's most photographed&nbsp;events into a moment of cultural expression.
